Fractures (Open/Displaced)
Acute Urgent Care
Visible bone/deformity, Bony crepitus, Cortical disruption on X-ray, Loss of function

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-032
Reason for Visit: Severe trauma to the lower leg resulting in extreme pain, an obvious bend in the limb, and a wound over the bone.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient who sustained a fall down a flight of stairs, resulting in an obviously deformed lower leg with a skin wound overlying the fracture site. Urgent, on-site medical assessment and stabilization were required because an open fracture poses an immediate, high risk of deep bone infection (osteomyelitis) and neurovascular compromise. Waiting for regular clinic hours or attempting private transport without splinting would guarantee severe, permanent limb damage.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My leg is completely bent the wrong way, and the pain is so bad I think I'm going to pass out.
History of Present Illness: The patient fell down four wooden steps approximately 30 minutes ago, landing awkwardly on the left leg. Immediately upon impact, the patient experienced a sickening "crack" and excruciating, localized bony tenderness. There is a complete loss of function of the affected limb; the patient cannot bear any weight or move the ankle. The patient notes an obvious angular deformity of the mid-lower leg. The parent reports seeing a small, bleeding wound directly over the sharp bend in the bone. The patient feels highly nauseous and lightheaded from the severe pain.
Functional Status: Lying on the floor at the bottom of the stairs, unable to move.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of bone infection and compartment syndrome.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 118 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient is in agonizing pain, pale, sweating, and clutching the thigh above the injury.
Physical Exam: Inspection of the left lower leg reveals an obvious, severe angular deformity at the mid-tibial shaft. There is a 2cm full-thickness skin laceration on the anterior shin with a visible bone fragment protruding through the skin (open fracture). Severe swelling and early ecchymosis are extending from the injury site. Palpation (kept to an absolute minimum) elicits bony crepitus. Crucially, distal pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) are currently palpable, and capillary refill is less than 2 seconds. Distal sensation to light touch is intact over the foot. Tachycardia is present secondary to severe pain. Expected investigation results involve orthogonal plain radiographs demonstrating cortical disruption and displacement of bone fragments.
Environmental Observations: Clear access to the patient; stairs are narrow but manageable for a scoop stretcher.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. No analgesia taken yet.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Fractures (Open/Displaced) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Severe Soft Tissue Sprain - Ruled out/unlikely due to the obvious angular deformity, visible protruding bone, and bony crepitus, which definitively confirm a structural cortical break rather than a ligamentous injury.

Compartment Syndrome (Acute) - Ruled out/unlikely at this exact moment due to the presence of intact pulses, normal capillary refill, and normal sensation, though the risk remains extremely high in the coming hours as swelling peaks.

Pathological Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to the mechanism of injury (high-energy fall down stairs) being sufficient to break healthy bone, and the lack of a prior cancer history.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the unmistakable diagnostic tetrad for an open, displaced fracture: gross angular deformity, visible bone protruding through the skin, bony crepitus, and a complete loss of function. The presence of a skin break over the fracture site transforms this from a standard orthopedic injury into a surgical emergency due to the massive risk of osteomyelitis. An urgent home visit was critical to provide potent parenteral analgesia, perform a documented neurovascular exam, cover the wound with a sterile dressing, and coordinate an emergency ambulance response, as private vehicle transport without proper traction splinting would risk severing the underlying tibial artery.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Morphine 10mg stat for severe pain. Applied a sterile, saline-soaked dressing lightly over the protruding bone to prevent further environmental contamination. Kept the limb strictly immobilized in the position found. Immediately called 000 for Priority 1 ambulance transport for traction splinting and transfer to a trauma center.
Positive Behavioral Change: By keeping the leg completely still and not attempting to straighten it yourself, you protect the delicate nerves and blood vessels surrounding the sharp, broken bone edges from being permanently sliced.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics and orthopedic surgery team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. The parent is instructed to monitor the foot. If the foot suddenly turns blue, cold, or completely numb, alert the 000 dispatcher immediately, as this indicates the sharp bone has cut off the blood supply.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: You have a severe, open break of your shin bone. This requires immediate hospital imaging, intravenous antibiotics, and an operation to wash out the wound and fix the bone with metal hardware.
Clinical Course: The paramedics will place your leg in a special splint that gently pulls it straight to reduce the pain and protect the blood vessels during the ride to the hospital.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to move your leg, even a fraction of an inch. Focus entirely on taking slow, deep breaths to let the painkiller take effect.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I have covered the exposed bone with a sterile dressing to stop bacteria from getting deep into the marrow. The strong painkiller injection will take the sharp edge off the pain so the paramedics can safely apply a splint.
Actionable Steps: Let your parent pack your phone and charger. Do not eat or drink anything, as you will almost certainly be going into surgery today to have the bone cleaned and set.

